{#
tasks/_completed_page.html.twig — one page of a list's finished tasks, in the
Completed group of the pane. See TaskPane on why they come later.

The first page answers the group's own frame, `tasks-completed`. Each page ends
with a frame of its own for the next, holding the link that fetches it: a link
inside a frame loads into that frame, so "Show more" swaps only itself for the next
page and the rows above stay where they are.

Every page frame sends what happens in its rows to the pane (`target`), because a
tick answers with the whole pane; the link names its own frame to stay put.

Accepts: pane TaskPane · tasks list<Task> · next int|null, the next page's offset ·
offset int, this page's
#}
{% set params = pane.query.toParameters() %}

<turbo-frame id="{{ 0 == offset ? 'tasks-completed' : 'tasks-completed-' ~ offset }}" target="tasks-pane-frame" class="block">
    <ul class="mt-1 space-y-0.5">
        {% for task in tasks %}
            {% include 'tasks/_row.html.twig' with { task: task, pane: pane, params: params } only %}
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>

    {% if next is not null %}
        <turbo-frame id="tasks-completed-{{ next }}" target="tasks-pane-frame" class="block">
            <a href="{{ path('app_tasks_completed', params|merge({ offset: next })) }}"
               data-turbo-frame="tasks-completed-{{ next }}"
               class="mt-1 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-xs text-ink-muted hover:bg-hover transition-colors">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-[0.625rem]" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ 'tasks.section.more'|trans }}
            </a>
        </turbo-frame>
    {% endif %}
</turbo-frame>
